BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:“Hannah in Context: What Really Happened\, and Why Did it Matter
 ?” Lecture
DTSTART:20260917T183000
DTEND:20260917T200000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:e318a8f88a0c9706fee23f3f86138a@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Captain Michael Rutstein recounts the events leading to the ba
 ttle between schooner Hannah and HMS Nautilus\, Oct. 1775. Join Captain Mi
 chael Rutstein as he covers the events leading up to the famous battle bet
 ween the armed American schooner Hannah and HMS Nautilus in October 1775. 
 He will walk us step by step through the engagement and wrap up with the r
 epercussions of this event and its impact on the course of the Revolution 
 here on the North Shore. Michael Rutstein: After drafting and then relucta
 ntly abandoning a business plan to create a replica of Hannah\, Rutstein c
 ommissioned local boatbuilder Harold Burnham to build a representation of 
 the 1812 Salem privateer FAME in 2003. For 23 seasons\, FAME sailed out of
  Pickering Wharf in Salem to critical and popular acclaim. In April 2026\,
  FAME was sold to the Liberty Fleet of Tall Ships in Boston.\n\nSources: N
 orth of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/hannah-in-cont
 ext-what-really-happened-and-why-did-it-matter-lecture/
LOCATION:Salem Armory Visitor Center\, 2 New Liberty Street\, Salem\, MA\,
  01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/hannah-in-context-what-really-happened
 -and-why-did-it-matter-lecture/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Land & Sea Club
DTSTART:20260917T150000
DTEND:20260917T170000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:2a9393caacbd4b1d3ef6a7ea8804a9@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:SESSION 1 RIVER & MARSH ECOLOGY Land & Sea Club brings kids in
 to the working life of the Museum Shipyard to explore\, make\, and learn b
 y doing. For this six-week fall session\, we’ll head outside to investig
 ate the Essex River and salt marsh. Students will explore tides and water 
 quality\, identify plants and animals\, track invasive green crabs\, learn
  about the changing marsh ecosystem\, and develop practical skills for saf
 ely working around boats and the waterfront. This isn’t a classroom prog
 ram moved outside. We’ll use the Shipyard\, river\, marsh\, boats\, and 
 the world around us as the classroom. Ages 10-16 Tuesdays & Thursdays | 3
 –5 PM Six weeks | $75/week Maximum 15 participants No previous experienc
 e is necessary. Come dressed to be outside\, get dirty\, and participate. 
 Come be part of the crew. Love Land & Sea Club? Keep an eye out for future
  sessions. Additional programs may be offered throughout the school year b
 ased on interest.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northof
 boston.org/event/land-sea-club/2026-09-17/
LOCATION:Essex Shipbuilding Museum\, 66 MAIN ST\, ESSEX\, MA\, 01929
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/land-sea-club/2026-09-17/
END:VEVENT
