BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Yin Yu Tang: A Chinese Home Tour
DTSTART:20260917T111500
DTEND:20260917T161500
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:b54c42e18242de62988ce02d3627e7@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Step inside a 200-year-old Chinese home in Salem to experience
  Chinese art\, architecture and culture. For 200 years\, Yin Yu Tang stood
  in the rural village of Huang Cun in a mountainous region of southeastern
  China. Home to eight generations of the Huang family\, this house was dis
 mantled\, transported and re-erected at the Peabody Essex Museum in 2003 t
 hrough a partnership between PEM and the governmental authorities of Huizh
 ou\, in Anhui Province. Yin Yu Tang’s journey constitutes a landmark col
 laboration between Chinese and American architects\, artisans\, conservato
 rs\, engineers and museum experts. The home is presented as it was when th
 e family last lived there in 1982. Plan Your Visit Exercise care Please he
 lp us preserve the house for future generations by refraining from touchin
 g objects and the structure itself. Non-flash photography and videography 
 is permitted for personal use only. Tripods and selfie sticks are not allo
 wed. Please walk with care and watch out for uneven stonework and raised t
 hresholds. Store your belongings\, but keep your coat Yin Yu Tang is parti
 ally outdoors and may be cool in fall and winter. Strollers\, bags\, backp
 acks and umbrellas are not permitted in the house. Please leave them at th
 e coatroom or the cubbies by the Information desk. Our staff at the entran
 ce of the house can look after strollers. Pick up your audio guide in adva
 nce Please pick up your complimentary audio guide\, available in English a
 nd Mandarin\, at the Information desk 10 minutes before your visit. Please
  note: Plan to bring your own pair of headphones. Before your visit\, pres
 s #50 to listen to an introduction to the house. Once inside\, look for st
 one blocks with two-digit numbers. Press the number plus the play > button
  to hear the audio. Be prompt Please be at the doors to Yin Yu Tang five m
 inutes before your scheduled visit with your audio guide in hand. A staff 
 member will alert you shortly before the end of your time slot.\n\nSources
 : North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/yin-yu-tang
 -a-chinese-home-tour/2026-09-17/
LOCATION:Peabody Essex Museum\, East India Square\, 161 Essex Street\, Sal
 em\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/yin-yu-tang-a-chinese-home-tour/2026-0
 9-17/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Adult Lecture Series: Laying their Hands to the Spindle: Women’s
  Spinning Protests\, 1768-1770
DTSTART:20260918T120000
DTEND:20260918T130000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:885f623243fafc35c2c2f4b298c5f1@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Women\, Spinning\, and Resistance: Stacey Fraser In the years 
 leading up to the American Revolution\, New England women played an import
 ant role in resisting British policies through their everyday lives and wo
 rk. On August 31\, 1769\, forty-five women in Lexington\, Massachusetts\, 
 gathered for a spinning protest—one of more than sixty such demonstratio
 ns held throughout New England between 1768 and 1770. By spinning wool and
  linen yarn together\, these women demonstrated their ability to produce t
 he textiles needed by their families while rejecting reliance on imported 
 British goods. Join Stacey Fraser for a fascinating look at how women used
  their social roles\, domestic skills\, and community networks to particip
 ate in the growing resistance to British rule. Discover how these seemingl
 y everyday acts of spinning and boycotting became powerful forms of protes
 t in the years leading up to the American Revolutionary War. About the Spe
 aker: Stacey Fraser is Assistant Curator at the Scottish Rite Masonic Muse
 um & Library in Lexington\, Massachusetts. She recently curated an exhibit
 ion exploring Lexington’s role in the American Revolution and chaired th
 e museum’s 50th anniversary committee. Stacey also serves on the advisor
 y board of LexSeeHer\, a Lexington nonprofit dedicated to making women vis
 ible in history. Previously\, she served as Curator and Outreach Manager a
 t Lexington History Museums and as Co-Director and Educator at the Ipswich
  Museum\, among other New England institutions. Entry Included with Museum
  Admission| September 18th | 12:00 PM | Adult Event\n\nSources: North of B
 oston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/adult-lecture-series-la
 ying-their-hands-to-the-spindle-womens-spinning-protests-1768-1770/
LOCATION:Wenham Museum\, 132 Main St.\, Route 1A\, Wenham\, MA\, 01984
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/adult-lecture-series-laying-their-hand
 s-to-the-spindle-womens-spinning-protests-1768-1770/
END:VEVENT
