BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Piece-by-Piece: The Art of Assemblage\, Where 1 + 1 = 3
DTSTART:20260920T120000
DTEND:20261014T170000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:1bab9307d7d414f13855116887e64b@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Discover the magic when individual objects\, no matter how sim
 ilar or different\, are joined into an entirely new work at the Cove Galle
 ry in Rocky Neck August 27\, 2026 – September 20\, 2026 Cove Gallery Ope
 ning reception: Thursday\, August 27 from 5 to 8pm Gloucester\, MA — The
  act of assembly reveals\, to the artist\, new forms\, new relationships o
 f color and shape. Perhaps another unit can be placed over here\, or a new
  piece added there\, giving a fresh and unexpected whole. These works by f
 ive Rocky Neck members explore assemblage\, in its broadest sense. Using m
 aterials ranging from wire\, corrugated cardboard\, reclaimed wood and har
 dware\, to toy figures\, rocks\, and recycled flameworked glass the pieces
  become\, in the hands of the contributing artists\, surprising and deligh
 tful 3-D works. The artists in this show include: David Covert has been in
 volved in art making for nearly fifty years. Basically self-taught\, by vi
 rtue of continuing to draw\, paint\, and occasionally sculpt since the lat
 e sixties. Much of his work has been in the two-dimensional arts\, dominat
 ed by drawing and painting and printmaking. His training has been mostly i
 nformal\, interspersed with individual classes at Boston area art schools 
 including the Museum School of the MFA\, Mass College of Art\, and the lat
 e DeCordova Museum school. For more information see https://www.davidcover
 t.art/. Brian Murphy graduated from Rhode Island School of Design\, then o
 ff to NYC honing agency skills. He settled in Beverly\, Massachusetts\, wh
 ere he continues to work with ad agencies and individual clients. In betwe
 en he still finds time to create original artwork made from scrap material
 s. To learn more\, visit https://brianmurphyartanddesign.com/. Marion Powe
 rs is a mixed media artist and a painter who lives and works in Manchester
 -by-the-Sea. Her assemblages incorporate objects collected during her trav
 els. Her work begins with a found frame. She composes intuitively using an
  evolving collection of jewelry\, watch parts\, hardware\, book covers and
  other objects that she has collected over time. Some pieces have personal
  history\, while others are chosen for their beauty or character or the st
 ory they suggest. She transforms these elements into compositions I call 
 “jewels”. What was once discarded or put aside becomes precious. She c
 reates these works to encourage others to look for beauty in the ordinary 
 and overlooked objects and to recognize the potential in everyday objects 
 and to\, hopefully\, inspire curiosity\, reflection and a deeper appreciat
 ion for the unexpected. Marion earned her BFA from Massachusetts College o
 f Art and Design. Her work is held in private collections in New York City
 \, Cape Ann and beyond. Judy Robinson-Cox creates miniature shadowboxes\, 
 collages and small assembled sculptures using a variety of found objects a
 nd tiny plastic people. She creates these light-hearted artworks to make p
 eople smile and appreciate the world in which we live. She attended NYU Sc
 hool of Fine Arts and earned a BFA from the University of Connecticut with
  additional classes at Montserrat College of Art. For more information\, s
 ee https://judy.robinson-cox.com/. Beth Williams explores the qualities of
  glass and color. Using light and transparency in surprising ways along wi
 th layering color on color helps her to create unusual contemporary beads
 \, jewelry and small sculptures. She gleans inspiration from her gardens\,
  the ever-changing light on the ocean\, the seascapes that surround her 
 … anywhere colors collide in nature. To learn more\, see https://www.bet
 hwilliams.com/. About the Rocky Neck Art Colony Rocky Neck Art Colony (RNA
 C)\, a 501(c)3 non-profit organization\, nurtures excellence in the arts t
 hrough exhibitions\, workshops\, residencies and vibrant cultural events f
 or its members and the public. On picturesque Rocky Neck in Gloucester\, M
 assachusetts\, RNAC operates three exhibition spaces\, open to the public 
 for free: The Cultural Center at Rocky Neck (6 Wonson Street)\; The Salted
  Cod Arthouse (53 Rocky Neck Ave.)\, a partnership gallery and café\; and
  Cove Gallery (37 Rocky Neck Ave.)\, open seasonally in the former gallery
  of local artists Gordon and Judith Goetemann. Check the website www.rocky
 neckartcolony.org for hours\, openings and special events. For more inform
 ation\, see the website or call 978-515-7004. IMAGES (high-resolution imag
 es available on request)\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://
 northofboston.org/event/piece-by-piece-the-art-of-assemblage-where-1-1-3/2
 026-09-20/
LOCATION:Cove Gallery\, 37 Rocky Neck Avenue\, Gloucester\, MA\, 01930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/piece-by-piece-the-art-of-assemblage-w
 here-1-1-3/2026-09-20/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Pieces of Salem: An Anthology of Salem Voices
DTSTART:20260920T130000
DTEND:20260920T140000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:236b8075f1a3e1fef413dd6e3c9972@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Join Salem’s Poet Laureate J.D. Scrimgeour and PEM’s Direc
 tor of Learning and Community Engagement David Snider for the official boo
 k launch of Pieces of Salem: An Anthology of Salem Voices. Pieces of Salem
 : An Anthology of Salem Voices is like a stroll along a beach\, each step 
 bringing a new\, fascinating discovery — a rock shaped like an ear\, a s
 moothed bit of sea glass\, the intricate patterns on a glossy shell. Edite
 d by J.D. Scrimgeour\, the collection contains prose and poems from more t
 han 130 citizens who live or work in Salem. Each contribution keeps to a 4
 00-word limit\, a number chosen to commemorate the city’s quadricentenni
 al. Taken together\, the pieces celebrate a famed city marked by ancient t
 ragedy\, but brimming with vitality. A book signing and light reception wi
 ll follow the program. This book was commissioned and compiled as a part o
 f Salem’s 400th anniversary. This PEM Reads event is a part of PEM’s c
 elebration of Salem 400+.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https:/
 /northofboston.org/event/pieces-of-salem-an-anthology-of-salem-voices/
LOCATION:Peabody Essex Museum\, East India Square\, 161 Essex Street\, Sal
 em\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/pieces-of-salem-an-anthology-of-salem-
 voices/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The NAA invites you to the 25th Annual Art Auction Exhibition & Ev
 ent
DTSTART:20260920T110000
DTEND:20261022T170000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:cc4c0110e5aaa6b4ed08a97d16be12@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Newburyport Art (NAA) invites artists\, members\, collectors\,
  friends\, and art lovers to gather for a celestial celebration of 25 year
 s of creativity\, connection\, and community. This year’s Art Auction ex
 hibition is on display from Wednesday\, September 2nd until Saturday\, Oct
 ober 3rd. A ticketed auction party will be held on Saturday\, October 3rd
 \, from 4-8pm. Over 160 artworks are available for online silent auction b
 idding now until October 3rd @6pm. In the downstairs Sargent Gallery\, the
  exhibit includes NAA Member Artwork featuring photography\, mixed media\,
  sculpture\, oil\, acrylic and more. Upstairs in the Hartson Gallery is th
 e Collector’s Collection – showcasing artwork donated from private col
 lections\, including work by notable artists such as Harold Feinstein\, Ca
 ra Weston\, and Carlos Mérida. For online bidding & tickets to our Octobe
 r 3rd party\, visit: Auctria.events./NewburyportArt25 Since 1948\, Newbury
 port Art has believed in the power of art to bring people together. For on
 e unforgettable event\, Saturday\, October 3rd\, from 4-8pm\, 65 Water Str
 eet will transform into a celebratory theme of a black\, white and silver 
 sky. The afternoon and evening offers a variety of ways to experience the 
 celebration including: live painting by Alan Bull and a chance to purchase
  his piece\; bidding on Members’ Artwork\, Collectors Collection\, and S
 ilent Auction Packages\; adding your own star and to an interactive commun
 ity constellation\; free tarot reading\; dancing to live jazz & swing with
  Jared Holaday\; sipping on a signature cocktail from the open bar\; enjoy
 ing catered food and dessert\; posing for your Moon Portrait in the sculpt
 ure garden\; and sharing your NAA story in an optional two-minute video in
 terview. NAA Member tickets: $70\; Non-member tickets: $90\; with a sugges
 ted attire of cosmic shine! Thank you to our lead sponsors\, Matter Commun
 ications\, Milestone 35 Tools & Woodworking\, and the Hylton Family Trust 
 for supporting our 25th Annual Art Auction. Gallery Hours: Wednesday throu
 gh Sunday from 11:00am–5:00pm. The gallery is handicap accessible. More 
 information\, as well as an online gallery is available at newburyportart.
 org.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/ev
 ent/the-naa-invites-you-to-the-25th-annual-art-auction-exhibition-event/20
 26-09-20/
LOCATION:Newburyport Art Association\, 65 Water St\, Newburyport\, MA\, 01
 950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/the-naa-invites-you-to-the-25th-annual
 -art-auction-exhibition-event/2026-09-20/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Third World
DTSTART:20260920T193000
DTEND:20260920T220000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:f6b21a366f817e4ebf924013747712@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Doors 7:00pm Show 7:30pm 21+\, Positive ID Required Premium Se
 ating with cocktail service: $59.50 + $6.95 service fee Standard Seating w
 ith cocktail service: $54.50 + $6.95 service fee GA Standing: $30 + $6.95 
 service fee + $5 Day of Show Celebrating 40 years\, ‘Reggae Ambassadors
 ’\, THIRD WORLD is one of the longest-lived Reggae bands of all time\, a
 nd one of Jamaica’s most consistently popular crossover acts among inter
 national audiences. Mixing in elements of R&B\, funk\, pop\, and rock and
 \, later on\, dancehall and rap\, Third World’s style has been described
  as “reggae-fusion”. Singer Bunny Rugs described the band’s sound: 
 “Strictly a reggae band\, no. Definitely a reggae band\, yes.” Guitari
 st Cat Coore said of their music: “The hybrid of various types of music 
 is a natural thing because\, by growing up in Jamaica\, we know the direct
  roots of reggae and ska. At the same time we live in a country where you 
 get to hear Chuck Berry\, Fats Domino and all the R & B artists.” Bassis
 t Richard Daley said\, “We took roots reggae music and put branches on t
 op of it”. The genesis of Third World was originally formed in 1973 by g
 uitarist/cellist Steven “Cat” Coore\, keyboardist Michael “Ibo” Co
 oper\, drummer Carl Barovier and singer Milton “Prilly” Hamilton all o
 f Inner Circle\, along with bassist Colin “Oliver” Leslie. Tomorrows C
 hildren bass guitarist\, Richard Daley soon replaced Colin Leslie and Lesl
 ie teamed up with Darren Green to manage and organize the group. After a f
 ew months\, Barovier was replaced by Cornell Marshall\, and the group made
  its live debut that year at Jamaica’s independence celebration. They pl
 ayed in and around the Kingston club scene and on the hotel scene island w
 ide\, making a name for themselves as one of the few fully self-contained 
 bands around. With 10 Grammy nominations and catalogue of charted hits spa
 nning four decades\, Third World accolades include\, the 1986 United Natio
 ns Peace Medal\, 1992 and 1996 Jamaica Music Industry Awards for Best Show
  Band\, New York City Ambassador Award in November 2009 at Brooklyn Academ
 y of Music (“BAM”)\, 2011 Excellence in Music at the Atlanta Caribbean
  Festival\, numerous awards and accolades from Martin’s International Re
 ggae and World Music Awards\, in 2012 William Clark was awarded Ambassador
  at Caribbean American Heritage Awards in Washington DC\, “The Art of Re
 ggae Music” award for 40 years of quality music and dedication at the 20
 13 staging of Jamaica Jazz and Blues in Montego Bay\, the Keys to the Citi
 es of Atlanta and Key West\, National Black Arts Award of Merit 2013 and m
 ore.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/ev
 ent/third-world/
LOCATION:The Cut\, 177 Main Street\, Gloucester\, MA\, 01930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/third-world/
END:VEVENT
