BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Yin Yu Tang: A Chinese Home Tour
DTSTART:20260920T111500
DTEND:20260920T161500
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:348b35c4331540f255751dced34136@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Step inside a 200-year-old Chinese home in Salem to experience
  Chinese art\, architecture and culture. For 200 years\, Yin Yu Tang stood
  in the rural village of Huang Cun in a mountainous region of southeastern
  China. Home to eight generations of the Huang family\, this house was dis
 mantled\, transported and re-erected at the Peabody Essex Museum in 2003 t
 hrough a partnership between PEM and the governmental authorities of Huizh
 ou\, in Anhui Province. Yin Yu Tang’s journey constitutes a landmark col
 laboration between Chinese and American architects\, artisans\, conservato
 rs\, engineers and museum experts. The home is presented as it was when th
 e family last lived there in 1982. Plan Your Visit Exercise care Please he
 lp us preserve the house for future generations by refraining from touchin
 g objects and the structure itself. Non-flash photography and videography 
 is permitted for personal use only. Tripods and selfie sticks are not allo
 wed. Please walk with care and watch out for uneven stonework and raised t
 hresholds. Store your belongings\, but keep your coat Yin Yu Tang is parti
 ally outdoors and may be cool in fall and winter. Strollers\, bags\, backp
 acks and umbrellas are not permitted in the house. Please leave them at th
 e coatroom or the cubbies by the Information desk. Our staff at the entran
 ce of the house can look after strollers. Pick up your audio guide in adva
 nce Please pick up your complimentary audio guide\, available in English a
 nd Mandarin\, at the Information desk 10 minutes before your visit. Please
  note: Plan to bring your own pair of headphones. Before your visit\, pres
 s #50 to listen to an introduction to the house. Once inside\, look for st
 one blocks with two-digit numbers. Press the number plus the play > button
  to hear the audio. Be prompt Please be at the doors to Yin Yu Tang five m
 inutes before your scheduled visit with your audio guide in hand. A staff 
 member will alert you shortly before the end of your time slot.\n\nSources
 : North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/yin-yu-tang
 -a-chinese-home-tour/2026-09-20/
LOCATION:Peabody Essex Museum\, East India Square\, 161 Essex Street\, Sal
 em\, MA\, 01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/yin-yu-tang-a-chinese-home-tour/2026-0
 9-20/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Daily Guided & Self-Guided Tour of Hammond Castle Museum
DTSTART:20260921T090000
DTEND:20260921T153000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:2f5eaf57c8091c8489c6c23d7041e9@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Step inside the breathtaking home of one of the most prolific 
 inventors of the 20th century\, John Hays Hammond\, Jr. Hammond Castle Mus
 eum is a dynamic STEAM museum\,*blending its historical significance as bo
 th **a historic house museum and a lapidarium. Inside\, you’ll not only 
 discover ancient\, medieval\, and Renaissance architectural elements and a
 rtifacts—including stone archways\, sarcophagi\, grave markers\, and an 
 extraordinary collection of stone and marble works—but you’ll also fin
 d a vibrant museum offering fresh educational and cultural arts programmin
 g. Visitors can explore items from the archival collection that have not b
 een publicly seen in generations\, along with newly curated content and th
 e most up-to-date information on the provenance of many pieces in our coll
 ection. New this season\, the Museum features four temporary exhibits that
  explore John Hays Hammond Jr. as both an individual and a scientific inno
 vator. In the Natalie Hays Hammond Temporary Exhibit Room\, Curatorial Dir
 ector John Leysath presents Spinning Platters with Hammond: Inside the Wor
 ld of Vinyl Records\, on display from April 29th through August 31st. Begi
 nning September 10th and running through the end of the 2025 season on Jan
 uary 4th\, Leysath will showcase” Hammond in Italy: The Love Affair Betw
 een the Hammonds & Italian Culture.”Additionally\, the Inventions Exhibi
 t Room will highlight Hammond’s groundbreaking Videotape Technology duri
 ng the spring and summer months\, followed by Hammond’s ahead-of-their-t
 ime Advertising Signs in the fall and early winter. Listed on the National
  Register of Historic Places\, and a repeated Reader’s Choice Best of No
 rth Shore winner by Northshore Magazine and a Trip Advisor Traveler’s Ch
 oice award recipient for the last three consecutive years\,** you can trus
 t that your visit will be an unforgettable experience. New This Season: Ne
 w Daily Hours and Flexible Arrival Times for Self-Guided Tours Open daily 
 from 9:00 am to 3:30 pm\, with the last entrance at 2:45 pm. The Museum\, 
 Museum Shop\, and grounds close at 3:30 pm. Experience Hammond Castle Muse
 um at your own pace with our new flexible arrival times for self-guided to
 urs. Your ticket grants you entry anytime within designated periods\, ensu
 ring you can arrive stress-free and enjoy your visit to the fullest. Morni
 ng Session: 9:00 am – 11:45 am / Afternoon Session: 11:45 am – 2:30 pm
  Self-guided tours allow you to explore at your own pace. We recommend pla
 nning for at least 60 minutes to experience the Museum’s fascinating gal
 leries\, grounds\, and temporary exhibits. **Accessibility Note: Please be
  aware that at nearly a century old\, the Hammond Castle Museum is not han
 dicapped accessible due to many difficult and winding stairs on the campus
  and within the Museum\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://no
 rthofboston.org/event/daily-guided-self-guided-tour-of-hammond-castle-muse
 um/2026-09-21/
LOCATION:Hammond Castle Museum\, 80 Hesperus Avenue\, Gloucester\, MA\, 01
 930
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/daily-guided-self-guided-tour-of-hammo
 nd-castle-museum/2026-09-21/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:LIttle Audrey
DTSTART:20260921T191500
DTEND:20260921T210000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:2e6a3cc453a00e5e10534b1994003e@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Post-film Q&A panel: Michael Mailer\, director\, Jeff Thomas\,
  producer\, Robert Wahlberg\, actor Directed by Michael Mailer Produced by
  John Buffalo Mailer\, Michael Mailer\, and Jeff Thomas Synopsis Inspired 
 by the real-life story of Audrey Santo\, who died at age 23 in 2007 after 
 she was left in a vegetative state since childhood\, the film centers on a
  Vatican-ordered investigation into reported miracles surrounding the teen
 age girl with akinetic mutism. Runtime: 98 minutes Past Screenings: July 2
 5 World Premiere at Woods Hole Notable Cast Emily VanCamp Captain America:
  Civil War\, Brothers & Sisters\, Revenge Jennifer Esposito Crash\, Fresh 
 Kills\, Somewhere in Queens Josh Bowman Doctor Who\, Revenge\, Final Fanta
 sy (videogame series) Aidan Quinn Legends of the Fall\, Desperately Seekin
 g Susan\, Michael Collins\, The Lightkeeper Robert Wahlberg Southie\, Myst
 ic River and The Departed\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https:/
 /northofboston.org/event/little-audrey/
LOCATION:Shalin Liu Performance Center\, 37 Main Street\, Rockport\, MA\, 
 01966
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/little-audrey/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Painting at the Edge
DTSTART:20260921T160000
DTEND:20260921T180000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:90fc199c4cf750085d2eb185dc5751@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Synopsis Traveling mostly on foot\, by raft or canoe\, English
  artist Tony Foster paints landscapes in the remotest of areas often riski
 ng his life to bring awareness to wilderness destruction and climate chang
 e. David C. Schendel\, Writer/Director/Producer Joe Pavlo\, Producer/VFX A
 rtist | Ikuko Kobayashi\, Production Design/Graphics Francisco X. Rivera\,
  Editor | Moritz Hans Laub\, Composer From the Filmmaker As a documentaria
 n I am always looking for stories that have multiple levels of meaning. Ya
 nk Tanks was a clever way of dissecting the cultural truth behind the Cuba
 n Embargo. The Comedy Club was a not-so-subtle look at the corporatization
  of standup comedy and its effects on freedom of expression. Enduring Demo
 cracy explores economic opportunism and the dangers when that intersects w
 ith racism. And Painting at the Edge is a story about an eco-warrior that 
 shows us how to forge a new relationship to nature. On the surface these f
 ilms might seem as simple as “how do you keep an old car running without
  spare parts” but one level below there is a bigger question. I met Tony
  Foster when I was hired to produce a video about the opening of his museu
 m in Palo Alto\, CA. My dad\, Alfred was a master watercolour painter and 
 so when I saw Tony’s paintings\, I immediately recognized the talent in 
 the work. Watercolour is by far the most difficult and unforgiving medium 
 to paint in – believe me I tried! At his museum opening I was standing i
 n front of one of the largest paintings I’ve ever seen of the Grand Cany
 on. Someone next to me asked what I thought of the painting. I replied “
 it’s a masterpiece but the colors look very pop art.” “Well\, I used
  to make pop art!” I turned to see it was Tony Foster himself. From that
  moment we had a trusting relationship because I had dared to critique the
  master directly. After several years of discussions about the shape of th
 e film\, Tony and I decided to make the documentary about his life togethe
 r. As a filmmaker this has been the most challenging adventure I have ever
  embarked upon. Most film production teams don’t need to worry about whe
 re they are going to charge their batteries or how much water is needed fo
 r an 8-day canoe trip. Besides the challenge and rigors of filming in the 
 wilderness how do you tell a story about such a singular man? Tony was set
  on making the film while he was working in the wild\, so we packed the ca
 meras\, tents and sleeping bags and made a film. The planet stands on the 
 edge of an environmental collapse. We must change our relationship to our 
 only home or very soon it could be uninhabitable. Tony is the perfect mess
 enger for those of us looking for a better way and hopefully for those tha
 t need the message most.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://
 northofboston.org/event/painting-at-the-edge/
LOCATION:Shalin Liu Performance Center\, 37 Main Street\, Rockport\, MA\, 
 01966
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/painting-at-the-edge/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sunset Schooner Sail in Newburyport
DTSTART:20260921T173000
DTEND:20260921T200000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:666fe043e4bc5d9e12e69cffb74bff@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Set sail with us this September! Join the Merrimack River Wate
 rshed Council for a special Sunset Sail aboard the historic Schooner Ardel
 le. Departing from Newburyport Harbor on Monday\, September 21 at 5:30 PM
 \, we’ll take in the last of the summer sunsets together. This exclusive
  evening on the water will bring together MRWC’s donors\, supporters\, a
 nd friends for a memorable experience at the place where the Merrimack Riv
 er meets the sea. The Sunset Sail is more than a beautiful evening aboard 
 a historic schooner. It is a chance to gather in community\, celebrate the
  Merrimack River\, and look ahead to a cleaner\, safer future for Newburyp
 ort Harbor and the watershed we share. Guests can connect with MRWC’s le
 adership on the communities\, wildlife\, and working waterfronts that depe
 nd on clean water — and the shared responsibility we all have to protect
  it. This event also comes at an important moment for MRWC’s work in New
 buryport. After several years of concerning results in our water quality m
 onitoring by our team and volunteers\, we are launching an investigation i
 nto persistent high bacteria levels in Newburyport Harbor. This Sunset Sai
 l helps to support the science\, advocacy\, and community partnerships nee
 ded to better understand these challenges and work toward long-term soluti
 ons. Guests will sail aboard the Schooner Ardelle\, a beautiful historic v
 essel built and captained by Harold A. Burnham\, a master shipwright who h
 as built several prominent local schooners. The Ardelle is rarely chartere
 d out of Newburyport Harbor\, making this a unique opportunity to experien
 ce the lower Merrimack in a new way. All proceeds from the Sunset Sail ben
 efit MRWC’s mission to protect and restore the Merrimack River. We hope 
 you’ll join us for an inspiring evening as we explore the future of the 
 Merrimack together.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://north
 ofboston.org/event/sunset-schooner-sail-in-newburyport/
LOCATION:Newburyport Harbor\, 27 Rear Water St.\, Newburyport\, MA\, 01950
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/sunset-schooner-sail-in-newburyport/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
