BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Samara Joy Quintet
DTSTART:20260922T193000
DTEND:20260922T210000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:675da6ede2d08beeed9c18d7a7e90b@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Samara Joy has emerged as one of the most extraordinary voices
  in contemporary jazz\, achieving a level of acclaim and visibility rarely
  seen at such a young age. At just 26\, the Bronx-born vocalist has earned
  five GRAMMY Awards—including Best New Artist—along with an NAACP Imag
 e Award\, signaling her arrival as a defining artist of her generation. Wi
 th a sound rooted deeply in jazz tradition and a presence that resonates a
 cross generations\, Joy has become a powerful ambassador for classic Ameri
 can music in the modern era. A native of the Bronx\, Samara Joy became ent
 ranced by classic R&B as a child and cut her teeth as a singer in her chur
 ch’s gospel choir. And while her family history is deeply musical—her 
 grandparents helmed the Philadelphia gospel group the Savettes\, and her f
 ather\, the musician and songwriter Antonio McLendon\, has produced\, comp
 osed and arranged his own astounding original work—she didn’t delve in
 to the jazz tradition until college at SUNY Purchase. During her studies t
 here she won the 2019 Sarah Vaughan International Jazz Vocal Competition\,
  which introduced her to the larger jazz scene as a rising star to watch. 
 She was heard\, by audiences and critics alike\, as a masterful interprete
 r of jazz standards and a rightful heiress of the sound\, technique and ch
 arisma that defined her jazz heroines—including Vaughan\, Betty Carter\,
  Abbey Lincoln and Carmen McRae. Joy released her self-titled debut on the
  Whirlwind label in 2021\, followed a year later by Linger Awhile\, her br
 eakout Verve debut\, of which Down Beat said\, “With this beautiful reco
 rding\, a silky-voiced star is born.” The album earned her a Grammy for 
 Best Jazz Vocal Album in addition to a headline-making win for Best New Ar
 tist. A deluxe edition of the album was released\, as well as the EP A Joy
 ful Holiday\, which took home Grammys for Best Jazz Vocal Album and Best J
 azz Performance in 2025. Her new Verve album\, Portrait\, which Joy co-pro
 duced with the veteran trumpeter/bandleader and multi-Grammy winner Brian 
 Lynch\, showcases the intimate\, soulful chemistry she’s developed with 
 her touring band\, already earning her an NAACP Image Award this year for 
 Best Jazz Album. Portrait also spotlights her burgeoning gifts as a lyrici
 st\, in ingenious arrangements that meld her thoughtful words with music b
 y Charles Mingus\, Sun Ra and her late mentor Barry Harris.\n\nSources: No
 rth of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/samara-joy-quin
 tet/
LOCATION:Shalin Liu Performance Center\, 37 Main Street\, Rockport\, MA\, 
 01966
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/samara-joy-quintet/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:“American Admiralty Law in Salem and Boston\, 1800–1840\, and 
 Its Legacy” Lecture
DTSTART:20260923T183000
DTEND:20260923T200000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:54c22e0ec8f47d777e8e791df01b7d@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Judge William G. Young explores how Salem and Boston maritime 
 conflicts (1800–1840) shaped early American admiralty law post Revolutio
 n. Learn about the establishment of American Admiralty Law in Salem and Bo
 ston following the American Revolution\, tracing how early 19th-century ma
 ritime conflicts in these port cities shaped the development of American j
 urisprudence. The lecture will examine how disputes over cargo\, salvage\,
  and prize cases during the period tested and refined the young nation’s
  legal system. Judge Young will also discuss the pivotal role played by Ju
 dge John Davis in establishing practical precedents for handling these cas
 es — precedents that would go on to influence the broader framework of A
 merican admiralty law. About the Speaker: The Honorable William G. Young h
 as served as a Judge of the United States District Court for the District 
 of Massachusetts since 1985\, following eight years on the Massachusetts S
 uperior Court. A graduate of Harvard College and Harvard Law School\, he s
 erved as a U.S. Army officer before beginning his legal career as law cler
 k to Chief Justice Raymond S. Wilkins of the Massachusetts Supreme Judicia
 l Court. He later practiced law with Bingham\, Dana & Gould and served as 
 Special Assistant Attorney General and Chief Counsel to Governor Francis W
 . Sargent. An acclaimed teacher of evidence and trial advocacy\, Judge You
 ng has taught at Harvard\, Boston College\, and Boston University. He is a
 lso a prolific legal scholar and the author of several influential works o
 n evidence\, trial practice\, and federal jurisprudence.\n\nSources: North
  of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/event/american-admiralty
 -law-in-salem-and-boston-1800-1840-and-its-legacy-lecture-2/
LOCATION:Salem Armory Visitor Center\, 2 New Liberty Street\, Salem\, MA\,
  01970
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/american-admiralty-law-in-salem-and-bo
 ston-1800-1840-and-its-legacy-lecture-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Campbell McLean: War-Birds & Adverts (Artist Talk)
DTSTART:20260923T110000
DTEND:20260923T120000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:5ff5ae39bd63030ae795bb25652fca@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:Campbell McLean explores her queer-American experience by crea
 ting caricaturist paintings\, objects\, installations\, and videos. From o
 il on canvas\, papier-mâché to found object\, McLean’s unique aestheti
 c approach shines through in her sporadic ‘make-due’ energy that flows
  seamlessly between 2D and 3D forms. The resulting works are immersive and
  modular series and installations that read as both specific and universal
 izing\, personal and relatable.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: h
 ttps://northofboston.org/event/campbell-mclean-war-birds-adverts-artist-ta
 lk/
LOCATION:Montserrat College of Art\, 23 Essex St\, Beverly\, MA\, 01915
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/campbell-mclean-war-birds-adverts-arti
 st-talk/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:COME FROM AWAY!
DTSTART:20260923T140000
DTEND:20260923T160000
DTSTAMP:20260914T170850Z
UID:577dc4289439ad1f44ce58c73fcf3f@massachusetts-events
DESCRIPTION:COME FROM AWAY is a moving and uplifting musical based on the 
 true story of what happened in the small town of Gander\, Newfoundland\, o
 n September 11\, 2001. When 38 planes carrying nearly 7\,000 passengers we
 re unexpectedly diverted there\, the residents of this isolated community 
 opened their hearts and homes to the stranded travelers. What began as a d
 ay of global tragedy transformed into a powerful tale of compassion\, conn
 ection\, and humanity. With a vibrant\, folk-inspired score and a fast-pac
 ed\, ensemble-driven narrative\, COME FROM AWAY captures the tensions\, cu
 lture clashes\, and eventual camaraderie that unfolded over several unforg
 ettable days. This Tony® Award-winning musical celebrates the best of the
  human spirit and the extraordinary capacity for good in the face of crisi
 s.\n\nSources: North of Boston\nEvent page: https://northofboston.org/even
 t/come-from-away/2026-09-23/1/
LOCATION:North Shore Music Theatre\, Dunham Road 54\, Beverly\, MA\, 01915
URL:https://northofboston.org/event/come-from-away/2026-09-23/1/
END:VEVENT
